{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"hub","type":"text","text":"CAT12 — Hard to judge good enough\nRQ1 · finding","x":140,"y":-80,"width":400,"height":100,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"cases","type":"text","text":"Cases\nL1 L2 L4 S1 S2 S4 S5 S6","x":-220,"y":-80,"width":300,"height":70,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"memo_group","type":"text","text":"**Memos** (8) — Ctrl+hover a link to preview\n\n- [[S1M5]] — end-only feedback lets errors accumulate\n- [[S2M6]] — early interpretation locks representation choices\n- [[S4M2]] — worked examples unlock reasoning and granularity\n- [[S5M2]] — examples and public feedback calibrate detail\n- [[S6M2]] — justification replaces the compile button\n- [[L4M4]] — granularity and consistency stay hard to teach\n- [[L2M2]] — time pressure shrinks the evaluation loop\n- [[L1M6]] — two-stage assessment rewards correction","x":-440,"y":208,"width":520,"height":304,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"code_group","type":"text","text":"**Codes** (28) — Ctrl+hover a link to preview\n\n- [[L1-C05]] — trial and error with tutor feedback improves models\n- [[L1-C08]] — accepts multiple valid modelling solutions\n- [[L1-C09]] — two-stage delivery with feedback on both\n- [[L1-C10]] — grades completeness consistency and readability\n- [[L2-C04]] — evaluation criteria taught but class time runs short\n- [[L2-C05]] — lecturer sees students skip self-evaluation of models\n- [[L2-C16]] — revision allowed but only final submission is graded\n- [[L2-C21]] — lecturer prioritizes more time over buying better tools\n- [[L4-C13]] — consistency across model perspectives is vital\n- [[L4-C15]] — meaning matters more than notation symbols\n- [[L4-C17]] — students struggle with enough granularity\n- [[L4-C20]] — iteration means crossing out and restarting\n- [[S1-C18]] — validation rules not retained from course\n- [[S1-C22]] — feedback only at end not along the way\n- [[S2-C13]] — representing relationships is hardest part\n- [[S2-C16]] — first interpretation sticks hard to revise\n- [[S2-C18]] — good model is complete and simple\n- [[S4-C03]] — less confident starting without teacher example\n- [[S4-C04]] — worked examples teach reasoning not memorizing shapes\n- [[S4-C05]] — comparing detail levels showed what good enough means\n- [[S4-C06]] — personal quality checklist learned from feedback\n- [[S5-C01]] — sequence diagram notation and granularity get messy\n- [[S5-C04]] — drafts version one without waiting for demo\n- [[S5-C05]] — teacher examples calibrate acceptable detail level\n- [[S5-C08]] — comfortable sharing work-in-progress publicly\n- [[S6-C05]] — good enough means explaining why each element exists\n- [[S6-C09]] — vague briefs and role-play reveal conflicting requirements\n- [[S6-C15]] — no compile button means longer ambiguity","x":600,"y":208,"width":520,"height":864,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"explainer","type":"text","text":"What this means\n\nThere is no compile/run button for a diagram. Students struggle to tell when a model is “good enough.”","x":80,"y":-380,"width":520,"height":150,"color":"1"}
	],
